WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.113 --> 00:00:01.920
- [Narrator] If you need to get somewhere,

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.840
let's say a new building,

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.410
put it into Maps and Google
gives you a route in seconds.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.540
But behind that seemingly simple answer

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.810
is a complex system of
data collected from users,

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:16.290
satellites, cars, even camels.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.330
Today, more than 1 billion people

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.190
use Google Maps every month.

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:23.580
And in 17 years, it's evolved
from a desktop application

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.440
to a massive mobile platform

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.830
that continuously updates
information about a location.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.880
- We have over 150 million
people around the world

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.100
that are contributing information,

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:39.384
with 50 million updates a day
coming from the community,

00:00:39.384 --> 00:00:41.010
from the public sector.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.560
So think of the map as being alive.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.300
- [Narrator] But the
amount of data it collects

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.960
has also attracted criticism
from privacy experts.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.780
Here's how in Google's
quest to remap the world

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:55.590
it had to revolutionize the
technology we're used to seeing.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.753
This is the tech behind Google Maps.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.290
- You can search for a place,

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.170
like an address, an
intersection, almost anything.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:05.610
- [Narrator] When it
comes to digital mapping,

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:09.060
Google wasn't the first on
the scene, or even the second.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.700
But when Maps launched in the mid 2000s,

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.930
it had technology many
people had never seen before.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.630
- I remember the day
that Google Maps launched

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:20.760
with satellite imagery in the browser.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.422
Everybody just went and
looked at their own house.

00:01:23.422 --> 00:01:24.930
- [Narrator] Craig is a professor

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:27.480
in the department of geography
at Hofstra University

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.463
who studies data driven geotechnology.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.330
- No one had ever seen their
own house in satellite imagery,

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:36.480
much less in color
satellite imagery before.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.670
This was a new view on the world.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:40.380
- [Narrator] Maps'
success was partly due to

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.060
acquisitions it made early on,

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.370
like Where 2 Technologies and Keyhole,

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.190
that made mapping more
accessible to consumers.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:49.682
- By buying Keyhole, they got the software

00:01:49.682 --> 00:01:52.530
that it took to have a easy, ready to use,

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.900
satellite image reviewer
on a global scale.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.880
Where 2 Technologies provided them

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.850
a much better user interface
than was available elsewhere.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:01.530
And by combining those with just

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.600
the sheer amount of capital at Google,

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.873
allowed them to spend in ways
and develop those further

00:02:06.873 --> 00:02:08.670
that you didn't see before.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:10.740
- [Narrator] Maps
expanded its tech quickly,

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.380
opening it up to developers,
integrating street view

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.680
and launching Android
and iOS apps early on.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.660
But how exactly did all
of this come together

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.840
to get you from point A to point B?

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:22.740
Christopher Phillips,

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.810
the current head of Google's geo division,

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.630
gave us an inside look
on the Google campus.

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:31.110
- The foundational piece was
to have a real world model,

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.540
the digital truth of what's
going on in the real world,

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.460
including where streets are.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:36.960
- [Narrator] Let's take
a look at a real route

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.330
in San Francisco that
Chris walked us through.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:40.830
- [Christopher] Let's say
I'm in the San Francisco

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:42.690
fairy building, the farmer's market,

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.720
and I need to get to San
Francisco International Airport.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.010
- [Narrator] To get that real world model

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:48.180
Chris was talking about,

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.580
Google starts with
satellite and aerial imagery

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.440
to create an accurate map.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.320
This is layered together
with data from street view.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.470
To do this, they use a
process called photogrammetry.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.990
Using this and GPS data, they can pinpoint

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.300
the right coordinates for the image.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:06.090
If we take a quick detour
off our route to France,

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.280
you can see what the
layering process looks like

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:09.930
using the Arc de Triomphe.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:11.430
- Think of it as a big jigsaw puzzle

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.440
where we can put these images together.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.320
And we understand the
distance between images

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.870
and where they sit with
the actual location,

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:20.280
the GPS location.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:21.990
- [Narrator] Google keeps
a range of the cameras

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.030
that have been used over
time to capture imagery

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:27.120
at the street view garage
on its campus in California.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.550
- It all started with a 500 pound camera

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.830
that was forklift onto a van.

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:33.300
- [Narrator] You may have
seen a street view car

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:34.133
at some point,

00:03:34.133 --> 00:03:36.630
with a 360 degree camera on top.

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:38.940
Some versions are equipped
with LIDAR sensors

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:40.530
which measure the distance from the camera

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:41.880
to objects around it.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:44.880
They're also equipped with GPS
to get an accurate location

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.100
for exactly where the
camera is positioned.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.750
But to reach more remote places,

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:51.450
they've adapted cameras
to fit the terrain.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:53.700
Google has put cameras on backpacks

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.490
to get to places that cars can't go.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:04:00.000
They've also used transportation
like bikes and snowmobiles.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.640
In some rarer cases,
Google has gotten images

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.880
from camels, divers and even astronauts.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:08.130
Their newest camera can
be detached from cars

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:09.510
to be carried around.

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:11.190
- We graduate to our most recent,

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:14.999
which is a 15 pound, very lightweight,

00:04:14.999 --> 00:04:17.280
and that can be powered off of a phone,

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:20.580
versus a large computing
unit that's in the car.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:22.770
- [Narrator] This imagery
isn't just for looking at.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:24.810
It actually helps keep
their maps up to date,

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:28.440
by detecting changes since the
last time images were taken.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.420
- We are not just capturing
what a place looks like

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:33.120
but we can detect that a
business might have changed.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:34.980
We can detect new signs.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.830
There's now a stop sign here,
or a traffic light here.

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:41.880
So understanding new physical
attributes of the real world

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:43.920
is what we've been able to derive.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.900
- [Narrator] Once they have
all the information they need

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.790
to figure out what the map looks like,

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:51.840
Google layers on info about
traffic, route and businesses.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.690
To do this, they get data
from local municipalities,

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:58.230
public bus and train schedules
and businesses themselves.

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:00.690
This is footage from 2013 of the software

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:02.820
Google still uses to edit maps.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:05.670
A combination of machine
learning and map operators

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:08.070
edit the public data like road networks,

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:11.190
to match exactly what you
see on the layered imagery.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:13.410
Since 2013, Google says they've been able

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.660
to automate most of this process.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.660
So for Chris's route from San
Francisco's ferry building

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:19.680
to the airport...

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.600
- [Christopher] Right now, I can see that

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.580
it's gonna take 21 minutes.

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:24.750
- [Narrator] In order to predict how long

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:27.270
it takes to get somewhere
or how busy a place is,

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:30.150
Google relies on reviews,
contributed information

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:32.010
and anonymized location data,

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:33.570
and uses algorithms to comb through

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:35.220
historical traffic patterns.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:38.370
It looks at current trends based
on your anonymous location,

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:41.070
aggregated with millions of other users.

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:44.430
- So we can overlay the trend
with the current activity

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:45.990
to then create a prediction.

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:48.540
- [Narrator] The company also
uses your approximate location

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:50.700
and its mapping tools to target ads

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:52.200
and advertise businesses,

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.600
which is one of the ways
the platform makes money.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.120
- We have the ability for businesses

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.920
to actually promote themselves on the map.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.380
So whether you're searching
for a place to go,

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:04.500
and if it's relevant to the
people who are in that area,

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:06.690
we can show that as one of the results.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:08.400
- So far, Google has mapped more than

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.770
250 countries and territories,

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:12.450
but as it's added more data

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:15.060
and ingrained itself in
our lives and culture,

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:17.250
users and regulators have pushed back

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:18.870
and demanded guardrails.

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:22.203
- Privacy is a big issue with any kind of

00:06:22.203 --> 00:06:24.360
geographic technology.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:27.720
And Google maps is only so much more so

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:32.720
because Google Maps is down to
an individual street address

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:35.730
down to individual people
when we connect it to things

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:39.900
like your Gmail account or your
wallet or your wifi network.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:41.340
- [Narrator] Google has been criticized,

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.050
and in some cases taken to court,

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:45.270
for things like the amount
of data it collects,

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:47.430
street view images
showing private property

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:49.680
and grabbing data from
unsecured wifi networks

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:50.970
with street view cars.

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:53.160
Google says the data from you that it uses

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:55.830
to inform traffic and
busyness is anonymized.

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:59.610
- We use a technology treatment
called differential privacy.

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:02.580
Differential privacy
allows us to separate you

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:04.890
and your activity from what we aggregate

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.740
from millions of people as signals

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:10.080
to derive insights around like busyness

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:12.900
and how long the walk
or the drive may take.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:14.130
- [Narrator] But some data that is used

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:15.840
to tailor your personal map,

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:18.930
like noting that you've been
to a location before, isn't.

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:20.730
They say that that data is not sold

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:22.800
or shared with third parties.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:25.440
Google also says that it
gives users privacy controls

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:27.630
like toggling location history on or off,

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:30.930
searching in incognito mode and
auto deleting location data.

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:33.480
- We're always improving how
we manage the information

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:36.060
we capture to protect people's privacy

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:39.330
and make sure that we only have
the critical amount of data

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:41.520
to help power the most useful products.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:43.200
- [Narrator] With its
advances in technology,

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:46.200
its advertising priorities,
and even its criticism,

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:49.440
Google Maps has helped change
the way we navigate the world.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:53.100
- The biggest improvement
that Google Maps has made

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:56.340
is making geographic
information much more accessible

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:58.140
to a very large number of people.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:00.180
The biggest downside, there's some thing

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:03.450
paying much closer
attention to the intricacies

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:06.030
of every movement you make
when you carry your phone.

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:07.410
- [Narrator] Google
says the future of Maps

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:10.170
aims to be more
comprehensive and immersive.

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:12.720
- We're taking technology
where we can combine

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:16.020
and fuse together satellite
imagery, aerial imagery,

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:18.660
street view imagery, user contribution,

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:20.310
to create a photorealistic view

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:22.980
of what it might feel
like to check out a park.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:24.000
What's the vibe of it?

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.340
What's the vibe of it on a different day?

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:28.170
- [Narrator] Immersive
view is Google's next step

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:30.060
in using their database of information

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:32.103
and imagery to map the world.

